39.3 State Types and Data Flow
Task states perform work through Lambda, AWS SDK service integrations, activities, or HTTP endpoints. Flow states include Choice, Parallel, Map, Pass, Wait, Succeed, and Fail. Each state receives JSON input and
produces output. Input/output filtering is powerful, but large payloads and accidental sensitive-data
propagation create cost, quota, and security problems. Store large objects in S3 and pass references.
{
"StartAt": "ReserveInventory",
"States": {
"ReserveInventory": {
"Type": "Task",
"Resource": "arn:aws:states:::dynamodb:updateItem",
"TimeoutSeconds": 10,
"Retry": [{
"ErrorEquals": ["DynamoDB.ProvisionedThroughputExceededException"],
"IntervalSeconds": 2,
"MaxAttempts": 4,
"BackoffRate": 2.0
}],
"Catch": [{
"ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
"Next": "ReservationFailed"
}],
"Next": "ChargePayment"
},
"ChargePayment": {"Type": "Task", "Resource": "...", "End": true},
"ReservationFailed": {"Type": "Fail"}
}
}
39.4 Retry and Error Design
Retries should target transient errors with bounded attempts, exponential backoff, and jitter where
implemented by the called component. Do not retry validation failures or permanent business rejection. A
Catch path should record context, choose compensation or manual review, and produce a terminal
business status. Every task should define a timeout so a lost callback or stalled dependency cannot hold
the workflow indefinitely. Important Workflow-level exactly-once semantics do not guarantee that an external side effect occurs
exactly once. A task can time out after a payment provider completed the charge.
